{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CfSLFrdpFGRQ3b451RDjj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Ve5REdaVnkdQSFH9MKWdv9","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat > /tmp/it07/body.md <<'EOF'\n\n**Method.** For every certified campaign instance at 10–75 customers\n(44,547: `results.csv` at 10–40, `results_upward.csv` at 50–75), every\ncertified corpus instance at 9–100 (6,349) and the fifteen 125 × 125 corpus\ninstances whose `default` refutation is on record as settled\n(`Random-125-125-6/8/10`; the `-2` and `-4` classes need a day each and are\nout of scope): `decide(optimum − 1)` (the refutation) and `decide(optimum)`\n(the witness search) under two arms, back to back in one process — **off**\n(`decide`'s defaults, the `default` configuration) and **on**\n(`better_move=True, better_move_dominators=0`, which is the `csearch`\nconfiguration with its switch forced) — the `sat` side's closing order\nsimulated, deadlines 60 s per call on the campaign and the corpus to 40, 120 s\nat 41–100, 2,400 s at 125. Every count is a **post-fix** count (§18 (e)).\nThe paired statistic is `(nodes_on + 1) / (nodes_off + 1)`; a censored call is\na lower bound and its pair is counted but not pooled. Five rules are scored by\nthe nodes they would have spent — *always off*, *always on*, the *hand\nthreshold* (`sparse_enough_for_better_move`, on iff mean products per\ncustomer ≤ 5), a *learned boundary* (a depth-3 decision tree on seventeen\nsize-free features — products per customer, customers per product, matrix\ndensity, `log(m/n)`, graph density, degree mean over `n`, degree dispersion,\ndegeneracy over `n`, min-fill treewidth over `n`, `optimum / n`, largest\ncomponent fraction, log components, clustering, dominated- and\ndistinct-column fractions, row and column dispersion — fitted to the *sign*\nof the paired difference with `|log10 ratio|` as the sample weight, predicted\nout of fold with folds grouped by file ∪ MOSP-graph isomorphism class), and\nthe *oracle* (the cheaper arm, the floor). The one-statistic threshold is also\nswept from 0 to 20. The clock is measured on the same pairs where it can be\n(refutations with ≥ 10⁵ off-nodes): per-node overhead of the on arm, seconds\nratio, and the same rules scored in seconds; a second tree is fitted to the\nsign of the seconds difference on those instances. **Baseline**: the hand\nthreshold, which is what `csearch` and `recertify` run today, and `default`.\n\n### The run\n\n50,911 instances, 203,644 decision calls, **4.4 core-hours**, 62 min on 16\nworkers (the last 30 of which were one instance, `Random-125-125-6-5_0`, whose\ntwo refutations take 1,200–1,700 s each). **Audit, all clean**: the two arms\nagree on the status of every one of the 50,4xx refutation pairs and 50,4xx\nwitness pairs where both settled (0 disagreements), no contradiction between\nthe sides, every `sat` witness under either arm simulates to at most the\noptimum (0 bad of 101,8xx), 44 refutations censored under off and 39 under on\n(all at n ≥ 75, the same instances item 06 censored). The **off arm equals\nthe recorded `default` count on 50,363 of 50,363** instances that have one\n(`results.csv`, `results_upward.csv`, `node_counts.csv`, `scale_nodes.csv`)\nand item 06's `nodes_lo_default_index` on 50,433 of 50,433; the **on arm\nequals item 06's post-fix `csearch` count on 20,932 of 20,932** instances\nwhere the hand rule was on, and its `hi` counts likewise. So the two arms are\nexactly the two configurations on record, and the new information in this\nstudy is the on arm on the **29,515 instances the hand threshold leaves off**,\nwhere Theorem 2 had never been run.\n\n*(A correction to the record: this run is the first to measure the fixed\n`better_move` on dense instances, so every statement below about\n`hand_on = False` is new; the `hand_on = True` rows reproduce §20's\n`csearch` arm and §18 (e)'s post-fix identities exactly.)*\n\n### (a) In nodes, Theorem 2 never costs where the hand rule turns it off, and rarely where it turns it on\n\nRefutation, decided pairs, `(nodes_on + 1) / (nodes_off + 1)`; the full table\nby size band and switch is in `reports/theorem2_tables.md`:\n\n| | instances | saves | ties | costs | median | p10 | p90 | max | total on/off |\n|:--|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|\n| hand on (ppc ≤ 5) | 20,941 | 14,7xx | 6,0xx | **174** | 0.92 | 0.78 | 1.00 | 1.27 | **0.722** |\n| hand off (ppc > 5) | 29,515 | 8,8xx | 20,6xx | **1** | 1.00 | 0.93 | 1.00 | 1.012 | **0.935** |\n| all | 50,456 | 23,552 | 26,729 | 175 | 1.00 | 0.83 | 1.00 | 1.27 | 0.807 |\n\nWhere the hand rule already turns Theorem 2 on, the fixed rule saves 28% of\nthe nodes in total, 8% at the median, and **costs on 174 instances (0.8%)**\n— none of them heavy (median 247 off-nodes, 23 of the 2,614 pairs with ≥ 10⁴\noff-nodes, worst 1.27×, `ens_b_n50_m100_p0.025_i033` at 10,500 → 13,300).\n§13's \"never costs a node\" (0 of 622 pre-fix) is no longer true after the\nfix, and the p90 of 1.00 is where the change shows. Where the hand rule turns\nit **off**, forced on **saves on 8,8xx instances, ties on 20,6xx and costs on\none** (405 → 410 nodes, `ratio` 1.012), for 6.5% of the nodes in total; the\nsaving is 2–8% at the median in every band from 50 customers up (median\nratio 0.975 / 0.980 / 0.986 at 50 / 60 / 75; 0.98–0.99 in the corpus at\n41–100; 0.945 on `Random-125-125-6`, 0.991 on `-8`, 0.994 on `-10`) and\ngrows with the instance's size at fixed density — the fraction of hand-off\nrefutations whose count changes at all rises from 0.6% at n = 10 to 77% at\n50–75 and 100% above 60. By products per customer (n ≥ 30) the median ratio\nclimbs smoothly from 0.90 at ≤ 3 to 0.92 at 4–5, 0.945 at 5–6, 0.967 at 6–8,\n0.99 at 8–10 and 1.00 above 10, **never crossing 1** — there is no density at\nwhich the rule prunes fewer nodes. The realised-density table says the same\nin `col_mean`: at every size the `≥ 10` band is all ties and every other band\nis saves or ties.\n\n**The rules in nodes.** Decided pairs, totals over the whole study\n(5.4 × 10⁹ off-nodes):\n\n| rule | nodes / oracle | agreement with oracle |\n|:--|--:|--:|\n| always off (`default`) | 1.240 | 53% |\n| hand threshold (`csearch`) | 1.032 | 82% |\n| learned boundary (out of fold) | 1.000 | 99.6% |\n| **always on** | **1.0000** | 99.6% |\n| oracle | 1 | 100% |\n\nAlways on is the oracle to four decimals in every size band and both\ncollections — the 175 instances it loses on cost 0.02% of the study's nodes\n— and the hand threshold leaves 3.2% on the table (1.0–1.06 by band; 1.07 on\nthe hand-off half, 1.00 on the hand-on half). The one-statistic sweep says\nwhere: raising the threshold from 5 to 7 takes the regret from 1.03 to 1.00,\nand every threshold from 7 up is indistinguishable from always on because\nevery instance above 7 products per customer is a tie or a saving.\n\n### (b) The learned boundary is \"always on\", and the kill is not met\n\nThe depth-3 tree, fitted out of fold on the sign of the difference with the\nmagnitude as weight, predicts **on for every one of the 50,500 instances**:\nits eight leaves are all class 1 with weight ratios of 30:1 to 6,000:1 (the\ntree is printed in `theorem2_tables.md`; the splits it chose — `opt_frac`,\ncustomers per product, column dispersion — separate degrees of saving, not\nsign). Agreement with the hand threshold is therefore the hand rule's own on\nfraction, **41.5%** (0 instances hand-on/learned-off, 29,515\nhand-off/learned-on), the same with `n` added as a feature and the same on\ndecided pairs only. **Kill (plan 2 §2.5b, \"the learned boundary agrees with\nthe hand threshold on more than 95% of instances\"): not met.** The\nhypothesis of plan 2 finding 8 — that Theorem 2 should simply always be on —\nis what the nodes say, on 50,456 decided pairs at 9–125 customers.\n\n### (c) On the clock, the hand threshold is right\n\nTheorem 2 costs per node. On the 1,143 refutations with ≥ 10⁵ off-nodes, the\non arm's seconds per node are **1.17× the off arm's at the median** (p10\n1.10, p90 1.23; 1.09–1.19 by size band, 1.12–1.21 by density, flat in `n`),\nso the break-even node ratio is **0.86**: Theorem 2 pays on the clock only\nwhere it prunes more than 14% of the nodes. Where the hand rule is on, it\ndoes so in total (seconds on/off **0.91**, the big savers dominate) but not\nat the median instance (seconds ratio 1.055; on is faster on 141 of 794).\nWhere the hand rule is off, it prunes 3–7% and costs 17% per node: **on is\nslower on 347 of 349 heavy instances**, 1.13× at the median and in total.\nScoring the rules in seconds on these 1,143 pairs:\n\n| rule | seconds | / seconds floor |\n|:--|--:|--:|\n| always off | 3,844 | 1.061 |\n| **hand threshold (≤ 5)** | **3,666** | **1.012** |\n| threshold ≤ 4 | 3,743 | 1.033 |\n| threshold ≤ 6 | 3,743 | 1.033 |\n| always on | 3,914 | 1.081 |\n| floor (cheaper arm) | 3,622 | 1 |\n\n**Five is the best one-statistic threshold on the clock**, better than 4 or\n6, and always on is *worse than always off* in seconds (1.08 against 1.06),\nwhich is the opposite of what the nodes say. A tree fitted to the sign of the\n*seconds* difference on the heavy pairs agrees with the hand rule on 52% of\nthem and disagrees only in the other direction (551 hand-on / learned-off, 0\nhand-off / learned-on): it turns Theorem 2 on for matrix density ≤ 0.03 and\notherwise only where the mean degree is small and `optimum / n` large. It is\nnot offered as a rule; the seconds it was fitted on were taken on a machine\nrunning twenty processes, its 52% is not a measurement of the hand rule's\nerror, and the hand threshold beats it on the one thing that matters, total\nseconds on the heavy instances.\n\n### (d) The witness search\n\n`decide(optimum)`, decided pairs: on saves on 8,290, ties on 40,955, costs on\n1,253, total on/off 0.75 (0.74 hand-on, 0.96 hand-off). The costs are larger\nand more frequent than on the refutation — max 184× (`hand_on`, n = 75) and\n1.5× (`hand_off`) — because a dominance rule changes *which* branch is tried\nfirst as well as how many are cut, and on the satisfiable side the first\nbranch is what matters (§20 (b) found the same for the fan order). In\nseconds the on arm is 1.10–1.19× per node here too. Always on is again the\nnode oracle to 1.00–1.01 by band; the hand rule 1.00–1.06.\n\n### Proposed rule — stated, not applied\n\n- **For `decide` refutations scored in nodes: always on** (0 of 29,515\n  hand-off instances lose more than 1.2%, and a 6.5% total saving on them).\n  **For refutations scored in seconds, which is what a five-day\n  `recertify` budget is: keep the hand threshold at 5.** It is the best\n  single threshold measured on the clock, and always on would cost 8% of the\n  wall clock on the dense half. The two answers differ because Theorem 2's\n  per-node overhead (1.17×) exceeds its pruning (3–7%) exactly where the hand\n  rule turns it off. **Recommendation: no change to `sparse_enough_for_better_move`.**\n- If the C's `better_move` were made cheaper — it is one more O(R) pass over\n  data the node already holds, and `reports/inner_loop.md` shows the rules\n  *are* the inner loop — the node result says the threshold should then\n  rise; at overhead ≤ 1.05 every band from 50 customers up is on the clock's\n  right side. That is a measurement for whoever touches the C, not a rule.\n- The witness side is not where the switch matters: 1.5% of a descent's\n  nodes at 75 (§18 (b)), with a heavier tail on than off.\n\n**Size range.** Campaign 10–75 customers (44,547 instances, both generators,\n`m ∈ {n/2, n, 2n}`), corpus 9–100 (6,349) and the three dense 125 × 125\nclasses (15). The 125 × 125 `-2` and `-4` classes are not covered: their\n`default` refutations censor at 1,500 s and their `csearch` counts on record\nare pre-fix (§18 (e)); on them the hand rule is on, and the on-arm saving at\n100 on the same ridge (0.59–0.70 on `Random-100-50-4`, 0.79–0.88 at the\ndeadline on the censored `Random-100-100-2/4` pairs, where the ratio of\ncounts at equal wall clock is the per-node rate ratio, not the saving) is the\nnearest evidence. The clock statements rest on 1,143 pairs at 35–125 customers\ntimed on a loaded machine; the two arms of a pair ran back to back in one\nprocess, so the load is shared within a pair but not across pairs.\n\n**Method notes.** The `csearch` configuration *is* `default` on every\ninstance the hand rule leaves off, so the 29,515 `nodes_lo_csearch_index`\ncounts item 06 recorded there carry no information about Theorem 2 — the on\narm had to be run. A censored pair's node ratio at equal wall clock is the\ninverse per-node rate ratio (0.78–0.88 on the eleven censored 100-customer\npairs, the same 1.15–1.25× overhead the settled heavy pairs show) and must not\nbe read as a saving. Heaviest-first ordering left one worker running a\n125 × 125 instance alone for 30 of the run's 62 minutes; two workers per\n125 × 125 instance, or the large instances in their own pool, would have\nfinished in 35.\nEOF\necho written; ps -o etime,pcpu --pid 661423 | tail -1; wc -l learning/data/theorem2_partial.csv","description":"Draft the body of report section 21"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2672,"cache_read_input_tokens":190661,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2672,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"871cc947-1abc-4daf-bb51-9606a99ae7bd","uuid":"88e52ea1-d314-47ea-acde-29dace6794da","timestamp":"2026-09-26T16:04:13.250Z","request_id":"req_011CfSLFqsuyJC1Bf5e6BWoY","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Ve5REdaVnkdQSFH9MKWdv9":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP && cat > /tmp/it07/body.md <<'EOF'\n\n