unes more than 14% of the nodes. Where the hand rule is on, it\ndoes so in total (seconds on/off **0.91**, the big savers dominate) but not\nat the median instance (seconds ratio 1.055; on is faster on 141 of 794).\nWhere the hand rule is off, it prunes 3–7% and costs 17% per node: **on is\nslower on 347 of 349 heavy instances**, 1.13× at the median and in total.\nScoring the rules in seconds on these 1,143 pairs:\n\n| rule | seconds | / seconds floor |\n|:--|--:|--:|\n| always off | 3,844 | 1.061 |\n| **hand threshold (≤ 5)** | **3,666** | **1.012** |\n| threshold ≤ 4 | 3,743 | 1.033 |\n| threshold ≤ 6 | 3,743 | 1.033 |\n| always on | 3,914 | 1.081 |\n| floor (cheaper arm) | 3,622 | 1 |\n\n**Five is the best one-statistic threshold on the clock**, better than 4 or\n6, and always on is *worse than always off* in seconds (1.08 against 1.06),\nwhich is the opposite of what the nodes say. A tree fitted to the sign of the\n*seconds* difference on the heavy pairs agrees with the hand rule on 52% of\nthem and disagrees only in the other direction (551 hand-on / learned-off, 0\nhand-off / learned-on): it turns Theorem 2 on for matrix density ≤ 0.03 and\notherwise only where the mean degree is small and `optimum / n` large. It is\nnot offered as a rule; the seconds it was fitted on were taken on a machine\nrunning twenty processes, its 52% is not a measurement of the hand rule's\nerror, and the hand threshold beats it on the one thing that matters, total\nseconds on the heavy instances.\n\n### (d) The witness search\n\n`decide(optimum)`, decided pairs: on saves on 8,290, ties on 40,955, costs on\n1,253, total on/off 0.75 (0.74 hand-on, 0.96 hand-off). The costs are larger\nand more frequent than on the refutation — max 184× (`hand_on`, n = 75) and\n1.5× (`hand_off`) — because a dominance rule changes *which* branch is tried\nfirst as well as how many are cut, and on the satisfiable side the first\nbranch is what matters (§20 (b) found the same for the fan order). In\nseconds the on arm is 1.10–1.19× per node here too. Always on is again the\nnode oracle to 1.00–1.01 by band; the hand rule 1.00–1.06.\n\n### Proposed rule — stated, not applied\n\n- **For `decide` refutations scored in nodes: always on** (0 of 29,515\n  hand-off instances lose more than 1.2%, and a 6.5% total saving on them).\n  **For refutations scored in seconds, which is what a five-day\n  `recertify` budget is: keep the hand threshold at 5.** It is the best\n  single threshold measured on the clock, and always on would cost 8% of the\n  wall clock on the dense half. The two answers differ because Theorem 2's\n  per-node overhead (1.17×) exceeds its pruning (3–7%) exactly where the hand\n  rule turns it off. **Recommendation: no change to `sparse_enough_for_better_move`.**\n- If the C's `better_move` were made cheaper — it is one more O(R) pass over\n  data the node already holds, and `reports/inner_loop.md` shows the rules\n  *are* the inner loop — the node result says the threshold should then\n  rise; at overhead ≤ 1.05 every band from 50 customers up is on the clock's\n  right side. That is a measurement for whoever touches the C, not a rule.\n- The witness side is not where the switch matters: 1.5% of a descent's\n  nodes at 75 (§18 (b)), with a heavier tail on than off.\n\n**Size range.** Campaign 10–75 customers (44,547 instances, both generators,\n`m ∈ {n/2, n, 2n}`), corpus 9–100 (6,349) and the three dense 125 × 125\nclasses (15). The 125 × 125 `-2` and `-4` classes are not covered: their\n`default` refutations censor at 1,500 s and their `csearch` counts on record\nare pre-fix (§18 (e)); on them the hand rule is on, and the on-arm saving at\n100 on the same ridge (0.59–0.70 on `Random-100-50-4`, 0.79–0.88 at the\ndeadline on the censored `Random-100-100-2/4` pairs, where the ratio of\ncounts at equal wall clock is the per-node rate ratio, not the saving) is the\nnearest evidence. The clock statements rest on 1,143 pairs at 35–125 customers\ntimed on a loaded machine; the two arms of a pair ran back to back in one\nprocess, so the load is shared within a pair but not across pairs.\n\n**Method notes.** The `csearch` configuration *is* `default` on every\ninstance the hand rule leaves off, so the 29,515 `nodes_lo_csearch_index`\ncounts item 06 recorded there carry no information about Theorem 2 — the on\narm had to be run. A censored pair's node ratio at equal wall clock is the\ninverse per-node rate ratio (0.78–0.88 on the eleven censored 100-customer\npairs, the same 1.15–1.25× overhead the settled heavy pairs show) and must not\nbe read as a saving. Heaviest-first ordering left one worker running a\n125 × 125 instance alone for 30 of the run's 62 minutes; two workers per\n125 × 125 instance, or the large instances in their own pool, would have\nfinished in 35.\nEOF\necho written; ps -o etime,pcpu --pid 661423 | tail -1; wc -l learning/data/theorem2_partial.csv","description":"Draft the body of report section 21"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01Ve5REdaVnkdQSFH9MKWdv9":{"cwd":"/home/al/dev/MOSP"}}}
